course: dictionary
lesson: 2558
title: Deliberate: on purpose
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: When a word has more than one meaning, the sentence around it tells you which one to use. Look for clues. «Deliberate practice means working on purpose on the parts you cannot do yet, slowly and carefully.» The words on purpose tell you deliberate means done intentionally, not by accident.
explain: Deliberate can also describe how someone moves or speaks. Slow and careful is the clue. Deliberate is not the same as quick or messy. Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence: «"Cost an arm and a leg" means cost a lot of money; it is a deliberate exaggeration.» A deliberate exaggeration is one chosen on purpose, so the speaker knows it is not really true.
explain: As a verb, to deliberate means to think carefully about something, often for a long time, before deciding. In formal texts a jury or committee deliberates. Notice the clue: thinking and deciding are the important actions. A quick guess would not be deliberation.
fact: Deliberate means done on purpose, not by accident.
fact: «Deliberate» can also mean slow and careful, as in a deliberate step.
fact: As a verb, «deliberate» means to think carefully before deciding.
fact: «Strategic misrepresentation» means deliberate lying to win approval, for example hiding a project's real cost.
fact: A good check is to swap "on purpose" into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «Deliberate practice means working on purpose on the parts you cannot do yet.» What does deliberate mean here? | Lost and forgotten | Very quick and rushed | Done by mistake | *On purpose, not by accident
exercise: «"Cost an arm and a leg" is a deliberate exaggeration.» Does this phrase mean a real arm and a real leg? | Maybe, it depends on the weather | Only if you are a doctor | Yes, it means a real arm and a real leg | *No, it is an exaggeration chosen on purpose
exercise: A jury will deliberate before it decides. What does deliberate mean as a verb here? | To forget everything at once | To shout loudly at everyone | To run away quickly | *To think carefully before deciding
test: A deliberate step is one that is slow and careful. Which word means the closest to deliberate in this sense? | Quick | Lost | Noisy | *Careful
test: «Strategic misrepresentation» means deliberate lying to win approval. Why is this lying called deliberate? | Because it is in a fairy tale | *Because it is done on purpose to win approval | Because it happens by accident | Because it is a funny joke
exam: Which is an example of strategic misrepresentation? | Forgetting your PE kit | Hiding a project's real cost to win approval | Telling a joke at lunch by accident | *Hiding a project's real cost on purpose to win approval
